module gunbc.machine_intake_mtcollins1_boot_artifact

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String }

// WHICH ARTIFACT MT. COLLINS BOOTS, AS ONE AUTHORITY, because it was previously two and nothing
// joined them. The fetch module authored a published path ending
// ubuntu-24.04.3-live-server-arm64.iso and the attach module authored an image name spelled
// ubuntu-24.04.3-live-server-arm64.iso, as independent literals in different files. They agreed, and
// nothing required them to.
//
// WHAT THAT ADMITTED IS THE FAILURE THIS WHOLE LANE EXISTS TO PREVENT. Edit one and the converge
// path verifies the digest of artifact A, publishes A into the export, and then asks the controller
// to present artifact B — which, if B happens to be in the share, succeeds. Every step reports
// success, every read-back confirms, and the unit boots bytes nothing checked. There is no arm for
// it because no single operation is wrong; the defect lives in the seam between two that are each
// individually correct.
//
// THE PUBLISHED PATH IS NOW DERIVED RATHER THAN AUTHORED, which is what makes the divergence
// unrepresentable instead of merely detectable. A check that the path ENDS WITH the name would have
// been the §5 shape to avoid: it concedes the two are separately writable and then validates them,
// and it is satisfiable by editing whichever of the two the author was not thinking about. Composing
// the path from the name leaves nothing to compare.
//
// AND THE DECOMPOSITION IS THE HONEST ONE rather than a trick to collapse a pair. A published path
// genuinely fuses two facts of different ownership: WHERE this host's controller reads media from,
// which is a property of the export and moves when the export moves, and WHICH artifact is being
// served, which is a property of the release and moves when the release moves. They were only ever
// one string because someone wrote them as one.
data mtcollins1_boot_export_dir: NonEmptyStr = "/srv/bmc"

// THE COMPOSITION ITSELF LIVES IN THE FETCH OPERATION, not here, and this module deliberately
// exports only the two atoms. A composed path helper beside them would be a third row derived from
// the first two, and the operation composes anyway because it needs the DIRECTORY separately -- the
// ownership admission is about the directory while the write is about the path. Exporting both the
// atoms and their composition would put two spellings of one fact back in circulation, which is the
// condition this module was created to end.
data mtcollins1_diskless_image_name: NonEmptyStr = "ubuntu-24.04.3-live-server-arm64.iso"

